hifiberry-audiocontrol (0.20.0) trixie; urgency=medium

  * An external cover art endpoint can now hand the daemon the image itself,
    instead of a URL for the client to fetch. That was the gap that made the
    previous release's endpoints unusable for anything on a private network:
    the URL reached the phone or browser, which had no route to the host and
    no credential for it. An endpoint may send the image base64-encoded in an
    `images` entry's new `data` field, or set `localize` and let the daemon
    fetch its URL — with the endpoint's configured headers, which the
    metadata fetch never sent. Either way the bytes land in the image cache
    and are served from `/api/imagecache/`, like the artist images already
    were, and are rewritten for `X-Forwarded-Prefix` as an external URL never
    could be.
  * Pass-through remains the default. `localize` is off unless configured, so
    a publicly reachable provider is untouched: copying its images onto an
    appliance's disk to serve them again would cost storage for nothing.
  * The daemon decides an image's type from its bytes rather than from
    anything the endpoint claims, which doubles as a check that the bytes are
    an image at all. A URL that answers with an HTML sign-in page or a JSON
    error is a realistic failure, and storing that would serve a broken
    picture from a URL the daemon vouches for.
  * Failing to obtain artwork the endpoint reported is cached as an error for
    an hour, not as an absence of artwork for a week. The endpoint said there
    was a cover; not getting it is this daemon's problem and must not leave
    the track blank until the negative cache expires. A cached answer whose
    image files have since gone is treated as a miss and looked up again,
    rather than served as a URL that 404s.
  * `max_image_bytes` bounds a single image and at most eight images from one
    answer are considered. Passing URLs through cost a metadata fetch each;
    localising adds a download, a file and a check on every later read, so an
    endpoint answering with hundreds of entries could otherwise turn one song
    change into hours of work.
  * Images stored in the cache are now written through a temporary file and
    renamed. `store_image_with_expiry` created the destination and filled it
    in place, so a client reading an entry while it was being refreshed saw a
    partial file. This affects every caller, not only cover art.
  * A locally served image is now measured for grading instead of being
    treated as a filesystem path and failing to open. This had been silently
    giving every image served from `/api/imagecache/` the worst possible
    grade, which also affected Shairport cover art.

 -- HiFiBerry <support@hifiberry.com>  Thu, 03 Sep 2026 16:28:36 +0000

hifiberry-audiocontrol (0.19.0) trixie; urgency=medium

  * Cover art can now come from external HTTP endpoints configured at
    runtime, under `services.external_coverart`. Each endpoint is a URL
    template with `{artist}`, `{album}`, `{title}`, `{year}` and `{url}`
    placeholders, its own headers for credentials, and its own timeout and
    cache lifetimes. The response contract is fixed — a JSON body with an
    `images` array — so an endpoint that speaks something else needs an
    adapter in front of it rather than a mapping in this config.
  * An endpoint may declare itself slow, which is what makes a lookup taking
    tens of seconds usable. A slow endpoint is never on the critical path of a
    request: the REST cover art handlers answer it from cache only, and the
    daemon runs the real lookup in the background when the song changes,
    publishing the result as a `song_information_update` event once it
    arrives. A client that wants to pay the wait can ask for it explicitly
    with `?include_slow=true`, which is bounded by the endpoint's configured
    timeout.
  * Cover art providers are now queried in parallel and without holding the
    provider registry lock. The previous fan-out held a global mutex across
    every provider's network I/O, plus a metadata fetch per returned URL, so
    one slow provider stalled every cover art request in the daemon,
    including those for other songs.
  * Answers are cached in three classes with separate lifetimes: an image
    found, a confirmed absence of artwork, and a lookup error. An endpoint
    outage therefore stops mattering within the hour instead of blanking a
    track for the length of the negative cache.
  * `doc/websocket.md` records the `cover_art_source` values a configured
    endpoint produces. The set is open-ended by design; a client should keep
    treating any source other than `station_logo` as real artwork rather
    than matching a fixed list.

 -- HiFiBerry <support@hifiberry.com>  Thu, 03 Sep 2026 13:37:10 +0000

hifiberry-audiocontrol (0.18.0) trixie; urgency=medium

  * `/api/now-playing` reflects a cover art lookup instead of only the
    WebSocket. The enrichment used to publish the better image as a
    `song_information_update` event and stop there, so the REST response kept
    reporting the station's logo for as long as the stream played, and a
    client that reconnected and re-fetched now-playing regressed to the logo
    even after a WebSocket subscriber had already moved on. The lookup now
    writes into the player's stored song, which both surfaces are built from,
    so a client that only polls sees the same progression a WebSocket
    subscriber sees.
  * A cover art lookup that finishes after its song has moved on is dropped
    rather than applied. The lookup is a network round trip and a radio track
    can end while it is in flight; nothing checked before, so a late answer
    could put the previous track's artwork on the one now playing. The update
    carries the title it was looked up for, and is discarded if that no
    longer matches the song in place.
  * Cover art belonging to a song is never overwritten by a later lookup;
    only a placeholder is, identified by `song.metadata.cover_art_source`.
    This rule previously lived wherever a plugin happened to assign
    cover_art_url, so it applied only where that plugin remembered to check
    it. It now lives once, in the player itself, ahead of every write.
  * What counts as a song change is now one rule for every backend: the title,
    the artist or the stream URL differs from what is already playing. Each
    backend previously carried its own version -- some compared the artist,
    some the album, some neither -- so what a client saw as a new track
    depended on which player it was listening to. Only two backends ever set a
    stream URL, so the artist has to be in the rule: without it a same-titled
    track by a different artist would not register as a change on MPRIS,
    Bluetooth, RAAT or Shairport, and the new track would be reported with the
    previous one's artist, album and cover art for as long as it played.
  * A metadata-only refresh of the song already playing -- cover art arriving
    after the song itself is the ordinary case -- now reaches clients from
    every source that speaks only when something has changed: the generic
    controller, Shairport, librespot, and RAAT's update channel. Several of
    those stored the refresh but the conversion on the way out would have
    dropped it, and some never attempted to propagate it at all. A source that
    instead re-delivers the current state continuously -- MPRIS and Bluetooth
    on a poll, RAAT's metadata pipe on every line it emits -- deliberately does
    not announce a refresh: what looks like one there is the same reading
    again, and announcing it would put an event on the bus per delivery for as
    long as the track played. Whether the source is polled or calls back makes
    no difference to this; whether it speaks only on a change does.
  * Cover art found by a lookup survives on RAAT. Every line down the RAAT
    metadata pipe carries the playback position and a full description of the
    track, so lines arrive throughout a track whether or not anything changed
    -- the player is declared gone after ten seconds without one. Storing each
    line's track unconditionally erased artwork a lookup had found within a
    pipe interval, and published a song change per line for the whole track.
  * A cover art lookup that names its source no longer relabels artwork it did
    not supply. `cover_art_source` was copied out of the lookup's answer even
    when the answer's image had been refused for belonging to the song
    already, which misreported to clients where the displayed artwork came
    from. Provenance now travels with the image or not at all.
  * `song.metadata.cover_art_source` gains the value `"enrichment"`, for
    artwork a lookup supplied without naming itself. The full set of values a
    client can meet is documented in the event contract, together with the
    rule for reading one it does not recognise: `"station_logo"` is the only
    placeholder, everything else is the track's own artwork.
  * Spotify playback no longer jumps back to the start of the track when the
    artist arrives late. The position was reset whenever the song's identity
    changed, and the artist became part of that identity in this release --
    but librespot announces a track first and fills in the artist moments
    later, so an ordinary track was rewound in the middle of playing. The
    reset now follows the track, not its metadata: when both the track playing
    and the one announced carry a Spotify URI, that alone decides, because it
    names the track exactly and does not change as the rest of the fields fill
    in. The URI is optional and can itself arrive late, so where either side
    lacks one the whole of the title, artist and album decides instead --
    which keeps two consecutive same-titled tracks apart rather than running
    the second one on from the first one's position.
  * AirPlay artwork is announced once rather than once per filesystem event.
    The cover art watcher sees a file created and then written, and each of
    those told every client to re-read a song whose artwork had not changed
    between them.
  * Shairport announces a session restart. `SessionStart` cleared the current
    song without telling clients, unlike `SessionEnd`, which did; a client
    that had a track showing when a new AirPlay session began kept showing it
    until the next song arrived. Both events now notify.
  * MPRIS and Bluetooth song changes now reach clients. Both backends rebuild
    their current song from D-Bus inside `get_song()` -- MPRIS also from a
    poll thread -- and neither emitted a change event from there before; MPRIS
    did not notify on a song change at all. An event now fires when the song
    genuinely changes, and only then: a poll that re-reads the track already
    playing stays silent and leaves the stored song alone, so cover art a
    lookup has found is not overwritten a second later by the same reading
    without it.

