hifiberry-audiocontrol (0.16.1) trixie; urgency=medium

  * A stop arriving in the first moments of startup is no longer ignored.
    The flag telling the signal handler to stand aside for the webserver was
    set immediately before launching it, but Rocket does not begin watching
    for signals there: it first builds and checks its router, runs its ignite
    fairings and sentinels, binds the port, and only then registers for
    signals. Through all of that the handler deferred to a server that was not
    listening, so the signal reached nothing and the daemon ran on until it
    was killed at the stop timeout -- the behaviour 0.15.1 removed, narrowed
    to a window rather than removed from it. A restart or package upgrade that
    stops the daemon just after starting it is the ordinary way to hit it.
  * The webserver is now prepared in two steps so the means to stop it exists
    before anything can ask for it, leaving no window in which a signal has
    nowhere to go. A startup that fails leaves nothing published, so a daemon
    whose API never came up can still be stopped, as before.
  * There is one signal handler in the process rather than two. The webserver's
    own is switched off and the handler in the main thread owns SIGINT, SIGTERM
    and SIGHUP throughout: while the server runs the signal is passed to it so
    its grace and mercy periods are honoured, and otherwise the handler ends
    the process itself. This replaces an arrangement that depended on the two
    handlers chaining in a particular order and on each standing aside at the
    right moment.
  * A stop asked for while the webserver is still starting ends the daemon
    rather than waiting for a shutdown that cannot arrive. The port is bound
    late in startup, so a signal sent while an outgoing instance still holds
    it is passed to a server that then fails to start, and nothing would have
    reported a shutdown that never began.
  * A stop asked for before the webserver starts stops it before it serves.
    The signal handler is registered early, and configuration loading and
    controller setup sit between that and the point where the main thread
    begins waiting -- seconds on a Raspberry Pi. A signal arriving in that
    stretch used to leave the main thread on its way out while the API thread
    went on to bind the port and serve, so process exit tore down a running
    webserver with no grace period and no shutdown fairings. The request is
    now seen before the server is launched.
  * The force-exit watchdog is armed by every shutdown signal. It previously
    sat on only one of the two paths, so a shutdown that stalled while the
    webserver owned the signals had no backstop at all. It now allows eight
    seconds -- longer than the six a graceful stop can take, shorter than the
    ten systemd allows before killing -- and the shutdown request is made
    before the watchdog is armed, so a failure to arm it cannot stop the
    daemon being asked to stop.
  * The webserver's shutdown grace and mercy periods are pinned rather than
    left to the defaults. They were readable from Rocket.toml in the working
    directory and from the environment, and the watchdog above is sized
    against them, so a longer grace set from outside would have had it force
    an exit in the middle of a shutdown that was proceeding normally.

 -- HiFiBerry <support@hifiberry.com>  Wed, 02 Sep 2026 13:49:14 +0000

hifiberry-audiocontrol (0.16.0) trixie; urgency=medium

  * Radio streams get the cover art of the track being played, not just the
    station's logo. A stream carries no artwork of its own, so the MPD backend
    fills cover_art_url with the station logo; that logo then blocked every
    better source. The Last.fm plugin fetches track info on each song change
    and already pulled the album image out of it, but assigned it only when
    cover_art_url was empty, which for a stream it never is. On a device
    playing ByteFM, Last.fm returned a 1200x1200 cover for the track on air
    while the daemon showed a 96x96 favicon. The better image is published on
    the WebSocket as a song_information_update; /api/now-playing is built from
    the player's stored song, which nothing writes back into, so it still
    reports the station logo.
  * Cover art that describes a URL rather than the track playing from it is
    marked in song metadata as cover_art_source "station_logo", and only such
    cover art may be replaced. The per-URL metadata cache is filled by the
    add_track API, which serves both the queueing of a radio station and the
    queueing of a single track with its own artwork, so the two are told apart
    by name: metadata naming something other than the track now playing -- a
    station name against a song title -- describes the URL. Names are compared
    without case or surrounding space, and an artist-less "Artist - Title" that
    was split earlier still counts as the same name. Where there is no name at
    all the URL decides: a stream's artwork is the station's, anything else
    belongs to the one track it was queued with. Artwork a client supplied for
    the track it queued is untouched, as is anything the player itself
    provided. Whatever replaces a placeholder records its own
    provenance, so a real cover is not taken for one by a later lookup.
    Clients that apply song_information_update as a partial update need no
    change; the event contract is in doc/websocket.md.
  * Last.fm answers the Song cover art method. Spotify was its only provider
    and returns nothing until an account is linked, so /api/coverart/song gave
    an empty result for every track on a device without Spotify. The lookup is
    unsigned and needs only an API key: the signed track.getInfo the scrobbler
    uses refuses without a linked account, which would have reproduced the gap
    it was added to close.
  * Cover art for the song being played no longer needs a linked Last.fm
    account either. The lookup that replaces a station logo used the signed
    request, so on a device with only an API key the logo was marked as
    replaceable and nothing ever replaced it. It now falls back to the unsigned
    request, which answers with the album and its images but not with whether
    the track is loved or how often it has been played -- fields that are
    absent from an unsigned answer and are no longer read as "not loved" and
    "never played".
  * The image chosen from a Last.fm album is the largest slot it filled, on
    both paths. Last.fm pads slots it has nothing for with an empty string, and
    the path that replaces a station logo took the extralarge slot alone, so an
    album whose extralarge slot was one of the empty ones left the station
    showing its logo. Both paths now share one selection.
  * The documented example for /api/coverart/methods listed providers that have
    never existed and now shows what the endpoint returns.
  * The 0.15.1 notes explained their own SIGTERM fix wrongly and are corrected
    here rather than rewritten. Their first entry said Rocket's signal handlers
    replace the earlier ctrlc handler, so it never ran; a later entry in the
    same release said, correctly, that Rocket registers through signal-hook,
    which chains onto the earlier handler rather than replacing it. The second
    account is the right one, and the reason SIGTERM went unhandled in 0.15.0
    is the one it gives: the ctrlc dependency's "termination" feature was off,
    so only SIGINT was watched. The same entry's "nothing in the process ever
    got the chance to flush its state" also implied the fix restores flushing.
    It does not: the process now exits cleanly instead of being killed, but no
    player is stopped and no cache is written out on the way.

 -- HiFiBerry <support@hifiberry.com>  Wed, 02 Sep 2026 13:17:01 +0000

hifiberry-audiocontrol (0.15.1) trixie; urgency=medium

  * audiocontrol exits when it is asked to stop. It never had: every stop,
    restart and package upgrade ended in SIGKILL after systemd's stop timeout,
    so nothing in the process ever got the chance to flush its state. The
    signal was not being ignored -- Rocket received it and shut the HTTP
    server down on time -- but the flag the main thread waits on was only
    cleared from a ctrlc handler that Rocket's own signal handlers replace as
    soon as the server launches. That handler had therefore never run, taking
    with it the force-exit watchdog written to catch exactly this, which sat
    inside it and could not fire. The main loop is now ended by the API server
    returning, which happens precisely when Rocket has finished shutting down.
    A stop takes about five seconds, which is Rocket's own grace and mercy
    periods, rather than the full ten-second timeout followed by a kill.
  * Starting the API server reports which of two things happened rather than a
    bare success. "Rocket ran and has shut down" and "the webserver is
    disabled, nothing was started" were the same value, so the caller had to
    re-read the configuration to tell them apart. Both are now distinct
    outcomes the compiler can check.
  * The startup log no longer claims "API server started on port 1080" from a
    thread that has not started anything. The webserver logs its own address
    when it binds one, which is the code that knows.
  * A shutdown that overruns Rocket's grace and mercy windows also ends the
    process. Rocket reports that case as an error rather than a success --
    outstanding background I/O, which long-lived WebSocket connections
    routinely produce, is one of its causes -- and treating it as a failure to
    start left the daemon spinning until it was killed, which is the behaviour
    the rest of this entry removes, on the shutdown paths a busy device is
    most likely to take.
  * SIGTERM is handled before the webserver starts, and when it is disabled
    entirely. The ctrlc dependency only registers SIGTERM with its
    "termination" feature, which was not enabled, so until Rocket launched
    there was nothing listening for the signal systemd actually sends. Once
    Rocket is up that handler stands aside: Rocket registers through
    signal-hook, which chains onto the earlier handler rather than replacing
    it, so both run on every signal and the earlier one would otherwise end the
    process during Rocket's grace period -- cutting in-flight requests and
    WebSockets after 150ms instead of shutting down over five seconds. The
    handover is symmetric: a launch that fails -- a port already held by a
    previous instance is the ordinary way -- hands the signals straight back,
    so a daemon whose API never came up can still be stopped.
  * SIGHUP shuts the daemon down gracefully rather than being ignored. It is
    one of the three signals the handler covers, so once the webserver is up
    Rocket is told to watch it as well; otherwise it would reach a handler
    that had stood down and no watcher, and a closed terminal would leave the
    daemon running.

 -- HiFiBerry <support@hifiberry.com>  Tue, 01 Sep 2026 14:33:56 +0200

hifiberry-audiocontrol (0.15.0) trixie; urgency=medium

  * Image and lyrics paths in every payload now carry the externally visible
    API prefix when audiocontrol is reached through a reverse proxy. Album
    cover art, artist thumbnails and the song paths on the WebSocket
    previously carried the internal path while the now-playing response
    carried the external one, so a client needed one rule per endpoint and
    per transport. A path that fell through the proxy answered 200 with the
    web interface's index page rather than an error.
  * List responses declare Vary: X-Forwarded-Prefix.
  * The library version token varies with the client's API prefix, both in the
    ETags on the album and artist lists and in the library_version field of
    /library/<player>. Without that, a prefixed and an un-prefixed response
    shared one validator for one URL, so a conditional request could be
    answered 304 for a representation the client never held, leaving it with
    paths wrong for the route it was on. The field remains opaque and is still
    compared for equality only.
  * A forwarded prefix that is not a plain path is refused, and paths then go
    out in their internal form exactly as they do when no prefix is reported.
    A value beginning with two slashes made every emitted path
    protocol-relative, so a browser resolved cover art against that host
    instead of the device, and a value carrying a query or fragment marker
    swallowed the rest of each path. Control characters are refused with them:
    a URL parser removes tab, carriage return and line feed before parsing, so
    a value containing one could otherwise be reassembled into either shape
    after passing the check.

